1 NAME Elisabeth Rachel /Merzbacher/
2 GIVN Elisabeth Rachel
2 SURN Merzbacher
2 _MARNM Kitzinger
2 NICK Rahel
1 NAME Elisabeth Kitzinger
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 12 APR 1881
2 ADDR
3 CITY Munich
3 STAE Kingdom of Bavaria
3 CTRY German Empire (Germany)
1 DEAT
2 DATE 14 JUN 1966
2 ADDR
3 CITY ,,
3 STAE Washington
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Social worker
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Elisabeth was the eldest of her parents' three children. Her father, Eugen Merzbacher, was a coin dealer and numismatist; her mother, Rosa Merzbacher (née Jaffé), was responsible for raising the children and runnin
2 CONC g the elegant household. After attending the secondary school for girls, Elisabeth Merzbacher led the life of a “house daughter”, supporting her mother and doing voluntary social work. She collected the small, unsupervised Jewish 
2 CONC children who were roaming the streets of the big city and supervised and taught them in her parents' apartment. This resulted in the Merzbacher private kindergarten for Jewish children in 1904, which quickly became very popular. T
2 CONC he institution was taken over by the Israelitische Frauenhilfe. A total of 150 children were admitted, although only two-thirds of them were Jewish - the city council had only approved the kindergarten and after-school care center
2 CONC  on the condition that children of all faiths would be admitted.
2 CONT
2 CONT On August 22, 1905, Elisabeth Merzbacher married the lawyer Wilhelm Nathan Kitzinger, who supported his wife in her voluntary work and was banned from practicing law in 1938.The marriage resulted in four children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth Kitzinger was of the opinion that Jewish welfare in Munich needed to be expanded much more extensively. That's why she got involved in the still young Israelitische Jugendhilfe (Jewish Youth Welfare Association), of whic
2 CONC h she was the primary chairwoman for many years, and advocated the establishment of an after-school care center, a girls' club, and a children's home, which took in children that were orphaned, abandoned, illegitimate or at risk i
2 CONC n their parents' home. Under her leadership, a girls' home for young girls who were working and a home for boys who had left school were set up.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1939, Elisabeth Kitzinger and her husband emigrated to what was then Palestine. Eight years later she moved to the USA to live with her son, the art historian Ernst Kitzinger.
