1 OCCU Social worker
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Background and Early Life:
2 CONT Born in Germany in 1908 to Wilhelm and Elisabeth Kitzinger, Gretl grew up in a prominent family. Her brother, Dr. Ernst Kitzinger, would later become a highly distinguished art historian and the director of the Dumbarton Oaks Rese
2 CONC arch Library and Collection for Harvard University.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gretl pursued higher education at the Ludwig Maximilian University of Munich (LMU), studying political economy (Wirtschaftswissenschaften).
2 CONT
2 CONT Social Work and Leadership:
2 CONT Rather than just focusing on academics, Gretl dedicated herself to social causes. During the highly volatile and dangerous years of the 1930s in Nazi Germany, she worked as a social worker for a Jewish welfare organization in Muni
2 CONC ch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her capabilities led her to a major leadership role: she became the head of the "Youth Aliyah for Germany" in Berlin, an organization focused on rescuing Jewish children from Nazi-occupied Europe and arranging their emigration to 
2 CONC Palestine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emigration and Later Life:
2 CONT In 1938, Gretl emigrated to Palestine, successfully escaping the escalating persecution in Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT A few years later, in 1943, she married Rabbi Dr. Robert Raphael Geis (1906–1972), a prominent German rabbi, theologian, and outspoken thinker who also managed to flee Germany. Sadly, very shortly after their marriage, Gretl had t
2 CONC o undergo a surgical procedure to remove a malignant tumor that proved fatal, and she passed away in 1943.
