2 CONT
2 CONT "New York, New York City Municipal Deaths, 1795-1949", database, FamilySearch (https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:2WR3-JC8 : 3 June 2020), Leon Meyer in entry for Leon Meyer, 1935.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Durmenach
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Durmenach,''' Haut-Rhin, Alsace-Champagne-Ardenne-Lorraine, France
2 CONT
2 CONT With the Treaty of Westphalia in 1648, Habsburg domination ceased and Durmenach became French.
2 CONT
2 CONT The village was an important Jewish settlement in the 15th century. Most of the houses in the centre were built by Jewish families between the 16th and 18th centuries.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1826, the Jews still lived in 66 different houses. Durmenach still had 650 Jews out of 1,000 inhabitants at that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1846, the Jewish population represented more than 56% of village.
2 CONT
2 CONT On February 29, 1848, the last antisemitic pogrom in France took place and it happened in the village and its surroundings.[4] It is also called Juden Rumpel or Judenrumpell. 75 Jewish houses were burned. An odonym (Rue du 29-Févr
2 CONC ier) commemorates this event.
2 CONT
2 CONT After 1940, most Jews had left or been deported and did not return after the Liberation.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jewish cemetery of Durmenach dates from the end of the 18th century[5] and at the time contained a thousand tombs, 300 of which are still visible.
